<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is Me! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is Me!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name elements of pictures. Interactive: Answer basic questions about familiar stories using simple words and gestures.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can name family members like "mom," "dad," or "brother" when asked supported with pictures. Can answer questions about family photos (e.g., "Who is this?"). Can respond to questions using simple words and gestures (e.g., pointing to "dad" in a photo when asked "Who is tall?").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Family Fingerplay" song. Students sing along and use gestures for "Daddy finger, daddy finger, where are you?" (for mom, brother, sister, baby). The teacher holds up large flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby) as they are sung, prompting students to point to the correct card. This activates vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "mom" and says clearly, "Mom. This is mom. My mom." The teacher points to themselves and then the flashcard. The teacher then models "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby" using flashcards and simple sentences, emphasizing the initial sounds /m/ in "mom" and /s/ in "sister" and the /b/ in "brother."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a 'mom' flashcard) "Is this a dog or a mom?" (Students respond with gestures or "Mom.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing two flashcards, 'dad' and 'sister') "Who is this?" (pointing to 'dad'). (Students respond "Dad.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful family poster or a short, simple picture storybook titled "My Happy Family" featuring cartoon characters representing mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, and a pet dog/cat. The visuals are prominent, and text is minimal and repetitive.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The teacher reads the story/points to the poster, clearly naming each family member and using simple sentences like "This is mom. She is happy." or "This is my brother. He likes to play." Students listen and observe, encouraged to point along. The teacher emphasizes pronouns "I, you, he, she, it" and possessive adjectives "my, your, his, her" through actions and repetition.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Point to":</b> The teacher asks, "Point to the baby!" or "Show me the happy dad!" Students point to the correct image on the poster/book.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Who is it?":</b> The teacher points to a family member on the poster and asks, "Who is it?" Students respond with a single word (e.g., "Sister!") or simple phrase ("My sister!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Yes/No" gestures:</b> The teacher asks, "Is this a cat?" while pointing to the dog. Students nod/shake heads or say "No."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Naming family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat). Using WH-questions ("Who is it?"). Using pronouns ("he, she") and possessive adjectives ("my, your, his, her"). Practicing pronunciation of /p/ in "play" and /b/ in "brother," and initial sounds /m/, /s/.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Choral Repetition with Action (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher uses large flashcards of family members. Teacher holds up 'mom' card and says, "This is mom. My mom." Students repeat in chorus, "This is mom. My mom." Teacher then asks, "Who is it?" Students respond, "Mom!" This is repeated for "dad," "brother," "sister," "baby," "dog," "cat." The teacher encourages actions for words like "play" (/p/ sound emphasized) and "love."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Teacher-Led Identification (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher has a set of smaller family picture cards. The teacher shows a card to a small group of students and asks, "Who is this?" Students take turns responding with the correct family member's name (e.g., "Dad," "Brother"). The teacher then expands by asking, "Is he happy?" (Students respond "Yes" or "No" with gestures/words). The teacher also introduces "She is happy" or "He is young" using the corresponding picture cards.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "My Family, Your Family" Pair Share (Guided Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of mixed family flashcards. One student picks a card and says, "This is my mom." The partner then asks, "Who is it?" and the first student answers, "My mom." Then roles swap. The teacher circulates, provides support, and models questions like "Is this your brother?" or "Who is tall?" (Students point and say "Dad!"). The teacher prompts use of "His mom," "Her brother" for more advanced students by demonstrating, "This is *his* mom" (pointing to the card held by the boy).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Family Show and Tell." Each student brings a simple drawing or a photo of their family (pre-assigned as homework, or provided with simple stick figures by the teacher). Students will present their picture to a small group or the class, naming their family members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in a circle with their family pictures.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes a turn holding up their picture and saying, "This is my family!" Then they point to specific members and say, "This is my mom," "This is my dad," "This is my brother," etc. (Minimum 2-3 members).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher models asking questions to the presenter: "Who is this?" or "Is your mom happy?" "Does your brother play?" (focusing on words like 'play' with /p/ sound).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Encourage other students to point and ask "Who is this?" to their peers if they can, or the teacher facilitates by repeating the question for them.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher prompts students to use `his/her` if appropriate (e.g., "This is *his* mom").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> During the "My Family Show and Tell," the teacher uses an observation checklist to note student performance. Each student is assessed on their ability to name at least two family members from their picture and respond to a simple "Who is this?" question.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Can pronounce "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister" clearly (e.g., /m/ in "mom," /s/ in "sister," /b/ in "brother").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Names at least two family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, baby, dog, cat).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Can be understood when speaking.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Attempts to answer questions from peers/teacher, or participates in group activity.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'mom' and 'dad'?" (Students give thumbs up or thumbs down, or point to pictures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I talk about my family?" (Students show happy face or sad face).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new family words can you say today?" (Teacher shows flashcards, students name them).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who do you love in your family?" (Students point to their family picture and say one name, e.g., "Mom!").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 8px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The use of personal family photos/drawings highly engaged the students. Choral repetition and TPR for "play" and "love" were effective.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students still need more practice differentiating "he" and "she." More structured pair work with sentence frames ("This is my ___") could be beneficial. I will ensure clear modeling of the /p/ and /b/ sounds.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Connection to next lesson: For the next Writing Composition lesson (Lesson 4), students will use the names of family members they spoke about today to start simple labeling or drawing activities, connecting spoken words to written representation.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>